From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Tue, 4 Oct 1994 06:29:58 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-10-53] SCI/COMP/CRYPT: Goodbye RSA, hello Quantum Computer

In terms of most everyday uses of RSA (such as in PGP for the
remailer network), declaring the "end of RSA" is premature.  
The technical problems, such as the need for practically atomic 
scale accuracy in the quantum dot, and the rapid collapse of 
the superposition before long keys can be factored, may mean a 
practical factoring machine might take quite a long time to
appear.  There's an outside chance that the NSA is building a 
quantum computer even now, but I heavily doubt that this 
will happen within the next ten years, and it might take much longer.  

If a few specialized agencies like the NSA develop quantum
computers, RSA will still protect messages from everybody else.  
It's not until quantum computing chips becomes widely avaiable that
that most of us need to replace RSA.  Also, commercial fabs
are now ahead of government contractors on the tech curve, so
it's doubtful that any sppoks will find out about the existance 
of practical fabrication capability for such quantum computers,
long enough before we do to actually build one in secret.

A big question is whether we can create a public key system 
that a quantum computer can't crack: technically speaking 
a polynomial function with quantum superpolynomial inverse
and polynomial trapdoor inverse.  There's no partcularly
good reason why such functions wouldn't exist, and the
task has now risen to the top of the the crypto theorists'
queues.

A positive aspect of this, that outweighs the blow to RSA
in the long run, is that several important machine
learning problems, such as PAC ("probably, approximately
correct") learning of finite automata, are as hard as
factoring.  By solving factoring, we can also solve a large
class of important induction taks in polynomial time.
Quantum computers thus might make great learning computers.  
The same result motivates the evolution of quantum computing
in the brain, although I suspect the brain environment 
is too messy to implement quantum computation.

Another area to watch for rapid improvements via quantum effects
is the the use of optically active polymers to create "optical
computing" elements.  A recent issue of _Science_, for example,
describes the fabrication and testing of an element that can 
compute a correlation of two 5000*5000 pixel pictures in one
step: the equivalent of 10^16 operations per second (7 orders of 
magnitude faster than current supercomputers).

