From: sw@tiac.net (Steve Witham)
Date: Tue, 4 Oct 1994 23:57:09 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-65] The pitfalls of futurology (fwd)

Nancy Lebovitz writes-

>This relates to something I've been mulling lately--I don't think that
>new ideas are developed in words--it can take living with them for
>a bit (or longer) before they can be put into words. What are they
>developed in?
>
>I get them as feelings before I can put them into words a lot of
>the time, but now that I think about it, the feelings relate to
>something being there, and various ideas or descriptions fitting or
>not fitting with it. This suggests that the idea may not exactly be
>the feeling, either.

(Third hand through a talk by Alan Kay:)

Someone in the middle of this century took a poll of physicists:  Do
you think in words, pictures, or what?

   Some, but not the best physicists, thought in words.
   Most thought in pictures.
   One said he felt sensations in his hands and arms--Einstein.

This is interesting when you consider his ability to write as well.

There are two patterns I notice in how I think.  One is, I say aloud what
I think the problem or solution or state of solution is.  Then I usually
say, "No, that doesn't sound quite right, the *real* problem is..." and
iterate like that.  The other is, I draw a diagram, and redraw and redraw
and redraw, trying to make it a perfect map of a problem.

 --Steve

