-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

 Anders Sandberg nv91-asa@nada.kth.se   on October 3, 1994 wrote: 
      
	>Acording to calculations made on the cryonics mailing list a
	>few years back, the necessary energy densities would vaporise
	the brain. Since synaptic structures are so small, high
	>frequencies would be needed. But the brain doesn't absorb them
	>very well, so the energy has to be rather intense.
	
I haven't read these calculations but I do know that absorption
is not the only thing that's important. In a conventional X- ray
photons have to be absorbed if we are to get any information out
of the object were looking at, this is because when the photon
hits the film only the position and to some degree the amplitude
of the wave is recorded, the phase of the wave is completely
lost. In a hologram the phase information is retained so even if
a photon is not absorbed by the target we can still get
information about the target, the photon could get diffracted.
That is , after all, how the DNA double helix was discovered and
they didn't even have X ray lasers or holography just a crude X
ray machine and a crystal to act as a diffraction grating.
	
	>I love the image of using gamma-ray lasers driven by atomic
	>explosions in space to image brains onto holographic film                   
	>moving at relativistic speeds.

The work of Dr. Rhodes with " hollow" xenon atoms ( Aug. 25 ,
1994 Nature) seems to indicate that desk top X-ray lasers in the
2 angstrom range should be practical in a few years, visible
light is about 7000 angstroms. A hologram could be recorded onto
a photorerefractive crystal or one of the newer polymers such as
poly-N-vinylcarbazole. You could even record it on regular X ray
film and view it with visible light, you'd just have to enlarge
it by 3500 ( 7000 angstroms / 2 angstroms). Dealing with film
that big would be a bit awkward but it beats moving it at
relativistic speeds.

An X- ray enlarger could be made with today's technology. Dr.
Nat Ceglio at Livermore made an X ray mirror using many thin
layers of Molybdenum and Silicon. Each layer doesn't reflect
much but  if the thickness of the layers are chosen properly at
one wavelength all the reflected waves coincide. The
reflection is coherent and about 60 % efficient. X- ray lenses
are also possible. M.A Kumakhov and Walter Gibson at the State
University of New York at Albany made one by bundling thousands
of tiny hollow glass capillaries that guide X-rays.
    
I don't claim to have proven this is a way to make a non
destructive upload but it is worth thinking about.

